“He said that it has been 10 days. I thought it had been just a few days.”

Sah was among hundreds of workers at a series of hydropower projects hit by the catastrophic August 26 flood, triggered by a .css-jfmysa{display:inline;word-break:break-word;} .css-1ankfgb{cursor:pointer;display:inline;word-break:break-word;} glacial mountain collapse on the China-Nepal border.

Sah’s extraordinary survival was a rare ray of hope after a disaster that killed at least 1,429 people and left more than 5,640 missing, the majority in Nepal.

Family, friends and neighbours celebrated Sah’s return with garlands and a cake, and his home was adorned with balloons and colours.

PORTLAND, Ore. (KOIN) – When tragedies and disasters strike around the world, Mercy Corps responds.

​The Portland-based humanitarian organization has delivered aid to tens of millions in more than 30 countries since its founding in 1979.

Now the non-profit organization has a new name, Prosper Global, but its goals remain the same.

​Its stated mission is to "Alleviate suffering, poverty and oppression" by doing everything from providing access to food and water to building basic infrastructure.

